        "say": "In Part 4 of Day 60, we execute Module 3 and Module 4 of the Grand Capstone: Ind AS 8 Retrospective Error Restatements vs Prospective Estimates, and Ind AS 10 Subsequent Events Accounting.\n\nModule 3 Mandate: Governing Accounting Changes and Historical Corrections:\nThe Global Financial Controller oversees the application of Ind AS 8:\n1. Retrospective Prior Period Error Restatement:\n   - Premature Revenue Recognition in FY 2024-25: $1,500,000.00;\n   - Section 115BAA Income Tax Shield (25.168%): $377,520.00;\n   - Net Reduction in Opening Retained Earnings: $1,122,480.00;\n   - Comparative Balance Sheet Impact: Restating opening trade receivables downwards by $1,500,000.00 and restating opening equity in the SOCIE downwards by $1,122,480.00;\n2. Prospective Change in Accounting Estimate:\n   - Revision of industrial machinery useful life from 5 years to 8 years;\n   - Reducing annual depreciation from $4,500,000.00 to $3,000,000.00;\n   - Delivering a prospective pre-tax operating profit saving of $1,500,000.00 in the current year and future operating periods with zero restatement to prior years.\n\nModule 4 Mandate: Navigating the Post-Balance Sheet Reporting Window:\nThe controller and statutory audit partner evaluate events occurring between March 31, 2026 and the Board authorization date of May 15, 2026:\n1. Adjusting Events Recorded in Financial Statements (Ind AS 10.8):\n   - Customer Apex Heavy Industries Insolvency: $2,500,000.00 receivable balance less $500,000.00 expected liquidation dividend requires an additional Expected Credit Loss (ECL) bad debt provision of $2,000,000.00;\n   - High Court Patent Litigation Settlement: Court decree requiring $1,800,000.00 settlement against existing $1,000,000.00 provision requires an additional litigation expense of $800,000.00;\n   - Total Pre-Tax Adjusting Loss: $2,000,000.00 + $800,000.00 = $2,800,000.00, recognized in draft financial statements with corresponding tax shield ($704,704.00);\n2. Non-Adjusting Events Disclosed in Explanatory Notes (Ind AS 10.10):\n   - Post-Balance Sheet Warehouse Fire Casualty: $3,500,000.00 inventory destruction at the Pune logistics hub disclosed in Note 33 with insurance claim details (zero balance sheet write-down);\n   - Proposed Equity Dividend (Ind AS 10.12): Board proposed dividend of $4,000,000.00 disclosed in Note 34 (zero liability recognized on balance sheet);\n   - Total Non-Adjusting Disclosures: $7,500,000.00.\n\nGoing Concern Solvency Certification (Ind AS 10.14):\nThe CFO and Lead Audit Partner perform comprehensive solvency stress testing:\n- Operating cash flows ($20,666,400.00) provide ample liquidity;\n- Liquid cash reserves ($10,000,000.00) cover short-term debt maturities by > 2.0x;\n- Current assets ($44.0M) exceed current liabilities ($22.0M) by 2 to 1;\n- Going concern status is certified as completely valid, robust, and verified.\n\nStatutory Audit Interlock and Cross-Module Consistency:\nBoth Module 3 and Module 4 working papers are cross-referenced with general ledger audit logs. The controller confirms that the opening retained earnings restatement (-$1,122,480.00), prospective depreciation saving ($1,500,000.00), and subsequent adjusting entries (-$2,800,000.00 pre-tax) are fully integrated into the master financial reporting platform without creating any trial balance imbalance.\n\nCorporate Governance and Audit Committee Oversight:\nUnder Section 177(4) of the Companies Act 2013, the Audit Committee must review with management and statutory auditors the annual financial statements before submission to the Board. The committee specifically interrogates the rationale for the useful life extension, verifies that the prior period error restatement was disclosed with complete transparency, and confirms that all subsequent events up to the date of the meeting have been classified in strict conformity with Ind AS 10. External credit rating agencies such as CRISIL and ICRA review the audited notes to confirm that the adjusting loss provisions and non-adjusting disclosures do not impair the company's AAA debt credit rating or breach financial leverage covenants. The committee unanimously recommends the audited statements for board approval.",
